<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.6">
  <!--
    Mcity mesh, rescaled 2026-08-21 from the 0.254 that shipped with
    osrf/car_demo to 0.127 == exactly half.

    WHY, AND WHY THIS IS NOT AN ARBITRARY "MAKE IT SMALLER"
      0.254 is the inches-to-metres constant, so whoever wrote the
      original assumed the .obj was authored in inches. Measured against
      real-world dimensions, that assumption is off by a factor of two:

                              at 0.254     at 0.127     real
        kerb reveal            0.316 m      0.158 m     0.15 m (US 6 in)
        town footprint      584x925 m    292x463 m     ~360 m across
        typical sidewalk      10.12 m       5.19 m

      The kerb is the decisive ruler here, and not for tidiness. The
      Husky's wheel radius is 0.1651 m. A 0.316 m kerb is 1.9x the wheel
      radius == a wall the robot physically cannot mount, so every kerb in
      town was an impassable barrier. At 0.127 the kerb is 0.158 m, just
      under one wheel radius: climbable with effort, which is exactly the
      real sidewalk-robot problem this project exists to study.

      So the rescale fixes a physics error and halves the map at the same
      time. It is not a cosmetic change.

    EVERY COORDINATE IN THIS WORLD IS TIED TO THIS NUMBER. Changing it
    again invalidates the spawn pose and all prop placements in
    mcity.world, and the sidewalk surface height they are measured from
    (now z = 0.158, was 0.316). Feature-richness measurements must also be
    redone == the camera sees the same geometry at a different distance.
  -->
  <model name="mcity">
    <static>true</static>
    <link name="link">
      <collision name="collision">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <scale>0.127 0.127 0.127</scale>
            <uri>model://mcity/meshes/mcity.obj</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <visual name="visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <scale>0.127 0.127 0.127</scale>
            <uri>model://mcity/meshes/mcity.obj</uri>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
      </visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
